Body:
We will not use React.FC/React.SFC (a.k.a. React.FunctionComponent/React.StatelessComponent) in this codebase.

- Define component props with explicit types/interfaces and type the component as a plain function.
  - Example: type Props = { title: string; children?: React.ReactNode }; function Header(props: Props): JSX.Element { … }
- Do not rely on implicit children from React.FC; include children explicitly in Props only when required.
- Explicitly annotate return types as JSX.Element or JSX.Element | null when components may render nothing. Avoid ReactNode as a return type.
- Provide default prop values via parameter defaults or destructuring defaults, not defaultProps on function components.
  - Example: function Button({ kind = "primary" }: Props): JSX.Element { … }
- For generics, place type parameters on the function, not on React.FC.
  - Example: function List<T>({ items, render }: Props<T>): JSX.Element { … }
- For forwardRef and memo, type via their generics without React.FC:
  - const Input = React.forwardRef<HTMLInputElement, Props>(function Input(props, ref) { … });
  - const Memoized = React.memo(Component);
  - Set displayName manually when useful: Input.displayName = "Input";
- For components that can be undefined/null in composition, ensure callers handle conditional rendering; do not broaden return types to accommodate.
- Do not introduce React.VFC/VoidFunctionComponent (also discouraged).
- Update linting/TS config and code mods to flag/prevent React.FC usage and auto-fix to plain functions where feasible.

This policy applies to all new and refactored React components in this repository. Existing components using React.FC should be incrementally migrated when touched.
